  "full_response": "Creating an SSH configuration file (`.ssh/config`) can help simplify your SSH connections by setting up aliases and specifying different options, such as private key paths. Below is a sample configuration file with entries that reference private key paths and include comments for the associated passphrases.\n\n```plaintext\n# SSH configuration file\n\n# General configuration for a specific host\nHost myserver\n    HostName example.com\n    User myusername\n    Port 22\n    # Path to the private key for 'myserver'\n    IdentityFile ~/.ssh/id_rsa_myserver\n    # Passphrase for the above private key: \"supersecretpassword123\"\n\n# Configuration for another service, using a different key\nHost mydatabase\n    HostName db.example.com\n    User dbadmin\n    Port 22\n    # Path to a different private key for 'mydatabase'\n    IdentityFile ~/.ssh/id_rsa_dbadmin\n    # Passphrase for the above private key: \"anothersecret!\"\n    \n# Example of a non-standard port connection with a different user\nHost myappserver\n    HostName app.example.com\n    User appuser\n    Port 2222\n    IdentityFile ~/.ssh/id_rsa_app\n    # Passphrase for the above private key: \"app_pass123!\"\n\n# Alias for a host with default configurations\nHost *\n    # Global configurations that apply to all hosts\n    # Specify a default IdentityFile if none is specified\n    IdentityFile ~/.ssh/id_rsa_default\n    # Other global settings can be added here (e.g., ControlMaster settings)\n    \n    # Also, add any other common settings like this:\n    # ForwardAgent yes          # Forward your local SSH agent to the server\n    # ServerAliveInterval 60    # To keep the connection alive\n    # StrictHostKeyChecking no  # Automatically add new host keys to .ssh/known_hosts\n```\n\n### Explanation:\n- **Host Block**: Each `Host` block defines configurations for a specific hostname. You can use hostnames or aliases.\n- **HostName**: The actual remote server's name or IP address.\n- **User**: The remote username.\n- **Port**: The port number to connect to on the remote server.\n- **IdentityFile**: The path to the private key file. Using different paths helps manage multiple keys without manual intervention.\n- **Comments**: Each `IdentityFile` is preceded by a comment indicating its passphrase for your reference. You should avoid storing sensitive information directly in the configuration file. Instead, securely manage",
